{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming fd9ab578. I reproduced the defect before promising rather than taking the description on trust, and it is worse than \"collision-prone\": a single commit forges a whole entry.\n\ninternal/gitstore/graph.go:30 frames records with %x1f between fields and %x1e between records. Four of those fields are attacker-controlled commit metadata - %s subject, %an author name, %b body, and the Rests-On trailer value - and git stores those bytes verbatim. I built a two-commit repository whose second commit body contains a record separator followed by a forged field layout, and ran the real Store.Graph against it:\n\n  parser returned 3 commits; git has 2\n    [0] hash=\"afcb83f22b5d\" subject=\"benign subject\" author=\"Honest Author\" time=1787392863\n    [1] hash=\"FORGEDHASH\"   subject=\"FORGED SUBJECT\" author=\"Evil Author\"   time=9999999999\n    [2] hash=\"023a77528dc1\" subject=\"ordinary commit\" author=\"Honest Author\" time=1787392863\n\nEntry [1] does not exist. Anyone who can land a commit message - which on this railway is anyone who can push a branch - can author rows in the graph with a hash, subject, author and timestamp of their choosing, and the far-future timestamp orders it wherever they like.\n\nTwo smaller faults sit behind it. A short record hits len(fields) \u003c 8 and is silently skipped, so a malformed or truncated commit disappears from the railway rather than announcing itself; and a timestamp that fails ParseInt leaves Time at zero without comment. Both fail open, quietly.\n\nWhat I will do: frame records so commit metadata cannot forge a boundary, and fail closed rather than skip when a record does not parse. The requirement to preserve ordinary Graph output semantics and refs handling means the change has to be invisible for honest history and loud for hostile history, and I will demonstrate both rather than assert them - the hostile repository above becomes a fixture, and the forged row must be gone rather than merely mangled.\n\nI will re-poll for any child request before committing. I collapsed a verdict into an assignment earlier today and it cost a round; the same mistake is available here.\n\nNoting this request is stale, so this promise and what follows are stale too."}